{"root":"pli/ms","translators":["sujato"],"title":"Free of Strife","html":"<article id='sn1.81'><header><ul><li class='division'><span class='root'>Saṁyutta Nikāya 1.81 </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Linked Discourses 1.81 </span></li>\n<li><span class='root'>8. Chetvāvagga </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>8. Slain </span></li></ul>\n<h1 class='sutta-title'><span class='root'>Araṇasutta </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Free of Strife </span></h1></header>\n<blockquote class='gatha'><p><span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>“Kesūdha araṇā loke, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>“Who in the world is free of strife? </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>kesaṁ vusitaṁ na nassati; </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Whose life is not wasted? </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Kedha icchaṁ parijānanti, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Who here completely understands desire? </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>kesaṁ bhojissiyaṁ sadā. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Who always lives as their own master? </span></span></p>\n<p><span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Kiṁsu mātā pitā bhātā, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>To whom do mother, father, and brothers </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>vandanti naṁ patiṭṭhitaṁ; </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>bow when they’re established? </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Kiṁsu idha jātihīnaṁ, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Who here, though low born, </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>abhivādenti khattiyā”ti. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>is bowed to even by aristocrats?” </span></span></p>\n<p><span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>“Samaṇīdha araṇā loke, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>“Ascetics are free of strife in the world. </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Samaṇānaṁ vusitaṁ na nassati; </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>The life of ascetics is not wasted. </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Samaṇā icchaṁ parijānanti, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Ascetics completely understand desire. </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Samaṇānaṁ bhojissiyaṁ sadā. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Ascetics always live as their own master. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Samaṇaṁ mātā pitā bhātā, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Mother, father, and brothers </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>vandanti naṁ patiṭṭhitaṁ; </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>bow to ascetics when they’re established. </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Samaṇīdha jātihīnaṁ, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>Even though an ascetic is low born, </span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>abhivādenti khattiyā”ti. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>they’re bowed to even by aristocrats.” </span></span></p></blockquote>\n<p class='endvagga'><span class='root'>Chetvāvaggo aṭṭhamo. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></p>\n<p class='uddana-intro'><span class='root'>Tassuddānaṁ </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></p>\n<blockquote class='uddanagatha'><p><span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Chetvā rathañca cittañca, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>vuṭṭhi bhītā najīrati; </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>Issaraṁ kāmaṁ pātheyyaṁ, </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></span>\n<span class='verse-line'><span class='root'>pajjoto araṇena cāti. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'></span></span></p></blockquote>\n<p class='endkanda'><span class='root'>Devatāsaṁyuttaṁ samattaṁ. </span><span class='translation' data-translator='sujato'>The Linked Discourses on Deities are complete. </span></p></article>"}
